    <!-- Introduction Section -->
    <section id="introduction">
      <h2>Introduction</h2>
      <p>"...knowledge of FHIR standards required."</p>
      <p>If you've been browsing job listings in the healthcare tech space, you've likely seen that phrase. But if you
        come from a background where HL7 was the dominant standard or if you’re newer to healthcare IT, it may not be
        obvious how FHIR relates to HL7. You may have knowledge of interfaces engines, programming skills like Python,
        JavaScript and JSON which can help you bridge the knowledge gap.</p>
      <p>In this post, I’ll walk through the key differences and overlaps between HL7 and FHIR, and how programming
        fundamentals can help you become fluent in both worlds. Whether you're a developer entering health tech or a
        clinician learning how data moves behind the scenes, this post will give some insight.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- What Is HL7 -->
    <section id="what-is-hl7">
      <h2>What Is HL7?</h2>
      <p>HL7 (Health Level Seven) is a family of standards for exchanging electronic health information. The most widely
        used version is HL7 v2, which has been around since the 1980s.</p>
      <p><strong>Key characteristics:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Event-driven messaging standard (e.g., ADT events, orders)</li>
        <li>Uses pipe-delimited plain text (e.g., <code>PID|1||123456||DOE^JOHN</code>)</li>
        <li>Hard to read, harder to debug</li>
        <li>Not built for the web or modern app architectures</li>
      </ul>
      <p>HL7 v3 was an attempt to solve some of these issues with XML and a stronger underlying model (RIM), but
        adoption was limited.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- What Is FHIR -->
    <section id="what-is-fhir">
      <h2>What Is FHIR?</h2>
      <p>FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) is a newer standard developed by HL7.org that builds on the
        lessons of v2 and v3 but embraces modern web technologies.</p>
      <p><strong>Key features:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>RESTful architecture</li>
        <li>Uses JSON or XML</li>
        <li>Resource-oriented (e.g., <code>Patient</code>, <code>Observation</code>, <code>Encounter</code>)</li>
        <li>Designed for APIs, mobile apps, and modern dev environments</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Where HL7 v2 focuses on events, FHIR focuses on <strong>resources</strong>: modular chunks of clinical data
        that can be easily created, read, updated, or deleted via HTTP.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- HL7 vs. FHIR: Where They Overlap -->
