      <p><strong>Real-world example:</strong> I once encountered a hospital lab system that used Visual Basic scripts to
        parse incoming HL7 messages from analyzers and route them into a central LIS (Laboratory Information System).
        The system had been in place for over a decade — no FHIR in sight, but the VB code still worked and was critical
        and risky to upgrade. This highlights the importance of understanding older tech even as we build with newer
        standards.</p>
      <p>No matter your language of choice, the key is understanding the <strong>data models, transmission patterns, and
          integration challenges</strong> of healthcare systems.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- The Role of Interface Engines -->
    <section id="interface-engines">
      <h2>The Role of Interface Engines</h2>
      <p>Interface engines are critical middleware tools in healthcare IT. They make sure data flows correctly between
        systems that often speak very different languages. They’re responsible for transforming, routing, and monitoring
        data as it moves across platforms, departments, and even institutions.</p>
      <h3>What Interface Engines Do</h3>
      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Function</th>
            <th>Explanation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Transformation</strong></td>
            <td>Convert HL7 v2 to FHIR, or vice versa; reformat XML/JSON messages</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Routing</strong></td>
            <td>Direct messages to the right system or endpoint based on content/context</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Validation</strong></td>
            <td>Ensure data conforms to expected formats and schemas</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Enrichment</strong></td>
            <td>Add missing fields by querying external databases or APIs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Monitoring &amp; Logging</strong></td>
            <td>Track message success/failure and system performance</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
