        <p>This diagram illustrates how different programming languages and technologies can work together in a hybrid
          stack to facilitate the transition from HL7 to FHIR.</p>
        <p>For example, you might use Python to parse and transform HL7 messages, then map them to FHIR resources, while
          Java handles the backend server logic and JavaScript powers the frontend web app.</p>
        <p>In this way, you can leverage the strengths of each language and technology to create a robust, interoperable
          system.</p>
        <p>In the real world, you might have a legacy system that still uses HL7 v2 messages, but you want to expose a
          FHIR API for modern applications. You can use an interface engine to handle the translation and mapping
          between the two formats.</p>
        <p>For example, a hospital might have a legacy EMR system that generates HL7 v2 messages for patient admissions.
          You could use Python to parse those messages, map them to FHIR resources, and then expose a FHIR API for
          modern applications.</p>
