    <!-- Why This Matters for Job Seekers -->
    <section id="job-seekers">
      <h2>Why This Matters for Job Seekers</h2>
      <p>When employers say they want "FHIR knowledge," they often mean:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You understand healthcare data structures</li>
        <li>You know how to work with APIs</li>
        <li>You can handle legacy data and move it forward</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you understand both HL7 and FHIR, you can:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Translate between systems</li>
        <li>Build middleware</li>
        <li>Validate and simulate data</li>
        <li>Improve interoperability at a system level</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You become not just a developer, but a <strong>bridge-builder</strong> – an added value in the job market,
        someone who can make systems talk to each other in a fragmented domain.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Final Thoughts -->
    <section id="final-thoughts">
      <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
      <p>You don't need to master every detail of HL7 or FHIR to be a valuable employee in this space.</p>
      <p>But you do need to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand the <strong>intent</strong> behind each standard</li>
        <li>Be comfortable with <strong>data transformation</strong></li>
        <li>Use your coding skills to make systems <strong>interoperable by design</strong></li>
      </ul>
